WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.650
- Apparently, there's a lot
of Bear Grylls fans out there.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.180
You've asked me to watch the scenes,

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.840
comment on their medical
accuracy and safety,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:07.860
and I'm about to do that.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:08.830
Let's get started.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.710
But first, a huge thank
you to Thrive Market

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.010
for sponsoring this video.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.760
- [Bear] You'll need water,
but if there isn't any,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.460
you can drink your own pee.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.320
- No!

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.930
Bear, come on.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:21.910
PSA, do not drink your own pee.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.620
If you drink what your body excreted,

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.130
especially if you're already
borderline dehydrated,

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.780
you're gonna be getting
rid of all the stuff

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.450
that you don't need and
then taking it back in.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.030
I know we sometimes say, urine is sterile.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:32.863
It's not.
(alarm blares)

00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:33.696
There's bacteria.

00:00:33.696 --> 00:00:34.650
Then you're gonna be drinking it?

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.270
Now you're giving yourself diarrhea,

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.300
potentially another fever-causing illness,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.100
which is gonna dehydrate you further.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.500
And there's other uses for your pee!

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.550
Put it on your clothes
and put it on your skin,

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.260
so that when it evaporates,
it cools you down.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.380
It might not smell great,
but you'll survive.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.213
Come on, Bear.

00:00:48.213 --> 00:00:49.741
- And if I just peed on the ground,

00:00:49.741 --> 00:00:51.890
that's all those fluids wasted.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.120
- They're not wasted
because fluid management

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.800
within your body is not just fluids.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.070
It's fluids plus-minus electrolytes,

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.690
and what your body does in urine is,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.000
it gets rid of those
electrolytes that it doesn't need

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.110
and it keeps a very careful balance.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.090
When you drink your urine,

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.840
you're essentially throwing that off

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.450
further and further each time.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.760
So, like, no. Do not drink urine.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.380
I'm gonna eat some Thrive seaweed

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.460
while he drinks that urine.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.393
Also salty.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.640
Much tastier.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.410
- Not, not my favorite.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.490
- No? That's not the
Gatorade flavor you go for?

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.340
- [Bear] Urine is actually 95% water,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.940
and it's sterile when fresh.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.660
- It's not, because it has
to go through your pee-hole,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.970
which sometimes has bacteria in it.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.380
- [Bear] And drink it
sooner rather than later,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.220
as it's a breeding ground for bacteria.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.230
- How does Bear Grylls say
that his urine is sterile,

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:42.310
meaning it has no bacteria,

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.120
then say it's a breeding
ground for bacteria?

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.430
Where does the bacteria come from?

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.600
- One thing you can do
if you're stuck out here

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.140
with no water source at all--

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.740
- Is not to drink your own urine.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.220
- Is actually drink the fluid
from a fresh elephant dung.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.103
- Oh, my God!

00:01:55.103 --> 00:01:58.570
Does he just try and find
liquid anywhere he can?

00:01:58.570 --> 00:01:59.527
- Pretty disgusting--
- Yeah, it is!

00:01:59.527 --> 00:02:01.530
- But it could save your life.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.970
- It's not,

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.030
All of this is so
temporary, it's incredible.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.720
There is so much bacteria in that water

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.690
that it will give you diarrhea,
thereby dehydrate you.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.470
This makes no sense.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:14.660
- Wow, I tell you what,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.420
these are the biggest
grubs I've ever seen.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.845
Look.

00:02:19.845 --> 00:02:22.360
And this is just the sort
of place that they like.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.290
They feed off all this rotting,

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.310
soft interior of the palm tree.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.450
- Just think about that.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:28.760
Do you want to eat a bug

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.100
that was just eating the
rotten interior of a palm tree?

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.870
- And actually, the
locals eat a lot of these.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.410
They're like a delicacy.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.260
They're full of good protein.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.240
- When he says "good
protein," does he mean it?

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.500
- [Bear] It'll be worth every
one of its 140 calories.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.200
- Cook it, my guy.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.443
- Holy smoke.

00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:49.122
That is

00:02:49.971 --> 00:02:50.804
just

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.090
horrible.
- Yeah!

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.440
- Do you know what?

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.060
They can keep their delicacies.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.150
- Like, I've seen people eat insects,

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.340
and crickets, and stuff before.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.260
They do have protein.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.613
But, like, the cooking
part is just mandatory.

00:03:08.003 --> 00:03:08.836
Is that a camel?

00:03:08.836 --> 00:03:12.053
- What I'm looking for is the stomach.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.420
- How does he know the anatomy of animals?

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.700
- It's in three parts,

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.440
so it can separate the
liquids from the solids.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.273
- Wow.

00:03:21.273 --> 00:03:24.400
- And the bit I want is called the rumen.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.340
- I see the fascia there.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.250
- [Bear] This rumen water
is just like a reservoir.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.290
- What? (laughs) Oh, my God.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:36.060
I can't imagine that
that is safe to drink.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.380
- And a nomad told me once how his uncle,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.160
in the 1950s, was lost
in a sandstorm out here,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.430
and he actually ended up killing his camel

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:50.460
to get at the rumen

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.790
and all this fluid inside.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:53.623
- Oh!

00:03:53.623 --> 00:03:54.720
- And that actually saved his life.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.370
- As an evidence-based physician,

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.550
I don't like hearing anecdotes like that,

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.950
that some guy, one time, 200 years ago,

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.570
saved his life by killing his camel.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.100
That, like, proves nothing to me.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.490
That story could've been made up.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.560
It could've been embellished.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.630
It doesn't mean that everyone
should be killing camels

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:10.120
and drinking their rumen water.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.000
I don't know, it just,
it sounds so extreme.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.463
- [Bear] And look inside.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.350
You can see everything it's been eating.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:18.403
